{"id":"neet-2026-q093","pn":93,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","st":"Phases of cell cycle","q":"Match List I with List II :\nA. G₁ phase — 1. Actual cell division occurs\nB. S phase — II. Cell is metabolically active and continuously grows but does not replicate its DNA\nC. G₂ phase — III. Synthesis of DNA occurs and the amount of DNA per cell doubles\nD. M phase — IV. Proteins are synthesized while cell growth continues\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III"],"c":0,"s":"In G₁ phase the cell is metabolically active and grows continuously but does not replicate its DNA.\nS phase is when DNA synthesis (replication) takes place and the amount of DNA per cell doubles.\nIn G₂ phase proteins are synthesised in preparation for mitosis while cell growth continues.\nIn M phase the actual cell division takes place.\nSo A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I."},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q106","pn":106,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","st":"Phases of cell cycle","q":"The correct sequence of adult cell cycle phases is ________.","o":["G1-M-G2-S","G1-S-G2-M","S-M-G2-G1","G1-G2-S-M"],"c":1,"s":"Gap 1 (G1) is the first phase of interphase.\nAfter G1, the synthesis (S) phase starts and DNA replicates.\nThen the Gap 2 (G2) phase occurs.\nFinally the cell cycle ends with M phase (mitosis).\nSequence: G1-S-G2-M.","sfig":{"w":385,"h":345,"alt":"Cell cycle circle: G₁ (largest sector), then S, then G₂, then a narrow M Phase sector divided into Prophase, Metaphase, Anaphase, Telophase and Cytokinesis; a thick arrow runs clockwise round the outer ring; cells leave the cycle at the top into G₀.","d":[["c",240,205,125,""],["c",240,205,108,""],["p","M 240 205 L 170.6 122.3 A 108 108 0 0 1 346.4 186.2 Z",""],["p","M 240 205 L 346.4 186.2 A 108 108 0 0 1 285.6 302.9 Z",""],["p","M 240 205 L 285.6 302.9 A 108 108 0 0 1 138.5 241.9 Z",""],["p","M 240 205 L 138.5 241.9 A 108 108 0 0 1 133.1 220.0 Z","f"],["l",104,216,161.4,224.6,"d"],["t",100,219,"Prophase","es"],["p","M 240 205 L 133.1 220.0 A 108 108 0 0 1 132.3 197.5 Z","f"],["l",104,202,159,207.8,"d"],["t",100,205,"Metaphase","es"],["p","M 240 205 L 132.3 197.5 A 108 108 0 0 1 136.2 175.2 Z","f"],["l",104,188,160.2,190.9,"d"],["t",100,191,"Anaphase","es"],["p","M 240 205 L 136.2 175.2 A 108 108 0 0 1 144.6 154.3 Z","f"],["l",104,174,164.9,174.7,"d"],["t",100,177,"Telophase","es"],["p","M 240 205 L 144.6 154.3 A 108 108 0 0 1 170.6 122.3 Z","f"],["l",104,160,177.1,154,"d"],["t",100,163,"Cytokinesis","es"],["p","M 130.5 244.8 A 116.5 116.5 0 0 1 165.1 115.8","B"],["p","M 163.7 96.1 A 133 133 0 0 1 263.1 74.0","Ba"],["t",260.3,149.2,"G₁","m"],["t",297.7,238.4,"S","m"],["t",214.9,262.8,"G₂","m"],["t",8,115,"M Phase",""],["c",137.6,91.3,9,"f"],["c",138.6,92.3,3.5,"F"],["c",170.5,68.7,9,"f"],["c",171.5,69.7,3.5,"F"],["c",261.3,53.5,9,"f"],["c",262.3,54.5,3.5,"F"],["p","M 299.3 45.5 A 10 10 0 1 0 285.3 43.5","a"],["t",291.3,55.5,"G₀","ms"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q109","pn":109,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","st":"Meiosis","q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement I : Chromosomes are fully condensed at the end of prophase I.\nStatement II : Meiosis I resembles mitosis.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","o":["Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is correct, but Statement II is false","Statement I is incorrect, but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are true"],"c":1,"s":"The last stage of meiotic prophase I is diakinesis.\nAt the end of prophase I the chromosomes are fully condensed and the meiotic spindle is assembled, ready to separate the homologous chromosomes.\nSo Statement I is correct.\nIt is meiosis II that resembles mitosis, as it is an equational division.\nSo Statement II is false."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q158","pn":158,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","st":"Mitosis spindle","q":"What is the main function of the spindle fibers during mitosis?","o":["To separate the chromosomes","To synthesize new DNA","To repair damaged DNA","To regulate cell growth"],"c":0,"s":"During mitosis the spindle fibres attach to the kinetochores of the chromosomes.\nThey help in the separation of the chromosomes."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q115","pn":115,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","st":"Metaphase","q":"Spindle fibers attach to kinetochores of chromosomes during","o":["Anaphase","Telophase","Prophase","Metaphase"],"c":3,"s":"Spindle fibres attach to the kinetochores of the chromosomes at metaphase."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q133","pn":133,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","st":"Meiosis prophase I","q":"Given below are two Statements:\nStatement I : Chromomes become gradually visible under light microscope during leptotene stage.\nStatement II: The begining of diplotene stage is recognized by dissolution of synaptonemal complex.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","o":["Statement I is true but Statement II is false.","Statement I is false but Statement II is true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"c":2,"s":"In leptotene the chromosomes slowly become visible under the light microscope.\nThe start of diplotene is marked by dissolution of the synaptonemal complex.\nAlso, the recombined homologous chromosomes of the bivalents tend to separate, except at the sites of crossing over.\nSo both statements are correct."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q159","pn":159,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","st":"Cell cycle phases","q":"Following are the stages of cell division :\nA. Gap 2 phase\nB. Cytokinesis\nC. Synthesis phase\nD. Karyokinesis\nE. Gap 1 phase\nChoose the correct sequence of stages from the options given below :","o":["B-D-E-A-C","E-C-A-D-B","C-E-D-A-B","E-B-D-A-C"],"c":1,"s":"The order is Gap 1 phase (E), then Synthesis phase (C), then Gap 2 phase (A).\nThen Karyokinesis (D), then Cytokinesis (B).\nSo E → C → A → D → B."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q179","pn":179,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","st":"Prophase I sub-stages","q":"Match List I with List II: (Sub Phases of Prophase I — Specific characters)\nA. Diakinesis — I. Synaptonemal complex formation\nB. Pachytene — II. Completion of terminalisation of chiasmata\nC. Zygotene — III. Chromosomes look like thin threads\nD. Leptotene — IV. Appearance of recombination nodules","o":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III"],"c":0,"s":"Diakinesis: terminalisation of chiasmata is completed.\nPachytene: recombination nodules appear.\nZygotene: synaptonemal complex forms.\nLeptotene: chromosomes look like thin threads.\nSo A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q111","pn":111,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","st":"Meiosis stages","q":"Which of the following stages of stages of meiosis involves division of centromere ?","o":["Telophase","Metaphase-I","Metaphase-II","Anaphase-II"],"c":3,"s":"The centromere splits in anaphase of mitosis and in anaphase II of meiosis.\nIn metaphase I and II, chromosomes only line up at the equator.\nIn telophase, chromosomes have already reached the poles."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q118","pn":118,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","st":"Meiosis prophase I substages","q":"The process of appearance of recombination nodules occurs at which sub stage of prophase I in meiosis ?","o":["Diakinesis","Zygotene","Pachytene","Diplotene"],"c":2,"s":"Recombination takes place in the pachytene stage of prophase I.\nThis stage shows recombination nodules."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q132","pn":132,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","st":"Phases of cell cycle","q":"Among eukaryotes, replication of DNA takes place in :","o":["G2 phase","M phase","S phase","G1 phase"],"c":2,"s":"DNA replication in eukaryotes happens in the S phase.\nMost cell organelles duplicate in the G1 phase."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q144","pn":144,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","st":"Phases of cell cycle","q":"Match List I with List II :\nList I: A. M Phase; B. G2 Phase; C. Quiescent stage; D. G1 Phase\nList II: I. Proteins are synthesized; II. Inactive phase; III. Interval between mitosis and initiation of DNA replication; IV. Equational division\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III"],"c":3,"s":"M phase is mitosis, the equational division: A-IV.\nIn G2, DNA synthesis stops but the cell makes RNA and proteins for the next phase: B-I.\nQuiescent stage is the inactive phase entered by non-dividing cells: C-II.\nG1 is the gap between mitosis and start of DNA replication: D-III."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q198","pn":198,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","st":"Stages of meiosis and mitosis","q":"Select the correct statements.\nA. Tetrad formation is seen during Leptotene.\nB. During Anaphase, the centromeres split and chromatids separate.\nC. Terminalization takes place during Pachytene.\nD. Nucleolus, Golgi complex and ER are reformed during Telophase.\nE. Crossing over takes place between sister chromatids of homologous chromosome.\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","o":["B and E only","A and C only","B and D only","A, C and E only"],"c":2,"s":"B and D are correct.\nA is wrong: tetrads form in zygotene.\nC is wrong: terminalisation of chiasmata happens in diakinesis.\nE is wrong: crossing over is between non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q200","pn":200,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","st":"Phases of cell cycle","q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I : During G0 phase of cell cycle, the cell is metabolically inactive.\nStatement II : The centrosome undergoes duplication during S phase of interphase.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","o":["Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect."],"c":0,"s":"Cells in G0 are metabolically active but do not divide unless needed. Statement I is incorrect.\nIn animal cells the centriole duplicates in the cytoplasm during S phase. Statement II is correct."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q125","pn":125,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","st":"Mitosis","q":"Which one of the following never occurs during mitotic cell division?","o":["Movement of centrioles towards opposite poles","Pairing of homologous chromosomes","Coiling and condensation of the chromatids","Spindle fibres attach to kinetochores of chromosomes"],"c":1,"s":"Pairing of homologous chromosomes happens in prophase I of meiosis.\nThe other three events occur in both mitosis and meiosis."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q131","pn":131,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","st":"Recombination nodules in pachytene","q":"The appearance of recombination nodules on homologous chromosomes during meiosis characterizes :","o":["Bivalent","Sites at which crossing over occurs","Terminalization","Synaptonemal complex"],"c":1,"s":"Recombination nodules appear in the pachytene stage of meiosis.\nThey mark the sites where crossing over takes place.\nCrossing over happens between non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q141","pn":141,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","st":"Types of chromosomes","q":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I: (a) Metacentric chromosome (b) Acrocentric chromosome (c) Submetacentric (d) Telocentric chromosome\nList-II: (i) Centromere situated close to the end forming one extremely short and one very long arms (ii) Centromere at the terminal end (iii) Centromere in the middle forming two equal arms of chromosomes (iv) Centromere slightly away from the middle forming one shorter arm and one longer arm\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)"],"c":3,"s":"Metacentric: centromere in the middle, two equal arms: (a)-(iii).\nAcrocentric: centromere near the end, one very short and one very long arm: (b)-(i).\nSubmetacentric: centromere slightly off the middle, one shorter and one longer arm: (c)-(iv).\nTelocentric: centromere at the terminal end: (d)-(ii)."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q167","pn":167,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","st":"Mitosis stages","q":"Select the incorrect statement with reference to mitosis:","o":["Spindle fibres attach to centromere of chromosomes","Chromosomes decondense at telophase","Splitting of centromere occurs at anaphase","All the chromosomes lie at the equator at metaphase"],"c":0,"s":"Spindle fibres attach to the kinetochores, not directly to the centromere.\nKinetochores are disc-shaped structures on the sides of the primary constriction (centromere).\nSo option (1) is incorrect."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q180","pn":180,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","st":"Meiosis","q":"Regarding Meiosis, which of the statements in incorrect?","o":["DNA replication occurs in S phase of Meiosis – II","Pairing of homologous chromosomes and recombination occurs in Meiosis – I","Four haploid cells are formed at the end of Meiosis – II","There are two stages in Meiosis, Meiosis – I and II"],"c":0,"s":"Meiosis has two sequential divisions, meiosis I and II, but only one round of DNA replication.\nThe short stage between them is interkinesis, with no DNA replication.\nSo there is no S phase in meiosis II."}
